{"language_code":"eng","language_probability":1.0,"text":"In 1637, a single tulip bulb could cost more than a fancy house in Amsterdam. But the story you heard about it? Mostly wrong. Tulips came from the Ottoman Empire and reached the Netherlands in the 1590s, and people went crazy for their colors. The most prized tulips had flame-like stripes. Nobody knew why. Today we know: a virus caused the pattern. One famous variety, called Semper Augustus, was said to cost thousands of guilders for a single bulb. Traders even sold bulbs that were still underground with paper contracts signed in taverns. In early 1637, prices shot up and some bulbs changed hands many times within days. Then, in February, at an auction in Harlem, nobody wanted to buy and the prices crashed. Legend says the whole country was ruined, with bankrupt families everywhere. But historians today found almost no proof. [laughs] Another legend: a sailor ate a rare bulb, thinking it was an onion. But that tale is only one anecdote and may not be true. In fact, towns let buyers cancel their contracts by paying just a small fraction, around three and a half percent. So the tulip mania was probably much smaller than the myth. The big story grew mostly from a book written 200 years later in 1841, and the Netherlands is still famous for its tulips today. So if you like history where the famous story is bigger than the truth, follow for more stories like this.","words":[{"text":"In","start":0.24,"end":0.4,"type":"word","logprob":-0.0007101159426383674},{"text":" ","start":0.4,"end":0.46,"type":"spacing","logprob":-7.748573807475623e-6},{"text":"1637,","start":0.46,"end":1.6,"type":"word","logprob":-0.000015925775549874288},{"text":" ","start":1.6,"end":1.78,"type":"spacing","logprob":-7.629365427419543e-6},{"text":"a","start":1.78,"end":1.82,"type":"word","logprob":-7.629365427419543e-6},{"text":" ","start":1.82,"end":1.98,"type":"spacing","logprob":-1.1920922133867862e-6},{"text":"single","start":1.98,"end":2